implementationPlan.description:
Phase 4b — G2, a terminal column may not strand a landing hold.

(a) state/taskStore.ts, updateTask: after `shouldClearWorker`/`finalUpdates` are computed and BEFORE `derivePlanRevision` (so it precedes every write and the hand-back close), insert
```
if (statusChanged && (normalizedUpdates.status === 'DONE' || normalizedUpdates.status === 'ARCHIVED')) {
  const finalizing = listAttempts(state, taskId).find((a) => a.phase === 'finalizing');
  if (finalizing) throw attemptFinalizingRefusal({ attemptId: finalizing.id, generation: finalizing.generation, taskId: finalizing.taskId, workerId: finalizing.workerId });
}
```
Import listAttempts from ./attemptStore.js and attemptFinalizingRefusal from ../util/claimGuards.js. Reuse that refusal rather than writing a new one: same code name, same context shape, and its message already names moe.finalize_attempt and the retryable rail.

Why the chokepoint and not set_task_status: every terminal transition funnels through updateTask — set_task_status REVIEW→DONE/ARCHIVED and the BLOCKED→ARCHIVED two-hop, archive_task (it allows REVIEW and BLOCKED), archive_epic, the board's UPDATE_TASK and ARCHIVE_DONE_TASKS. One guard covers them all; a guard inside set_task_status would leave the siblings stranding attempts exactly as today. qa_approve keeps its own earlier refusal, so its precedence and message are unchanged.

Only DONE and ARCHIVED are refused. REVIEW→WORKING/PLANNING/BACKLOG stay legal while finalizing: those rows are still claimable, the hold ends normally, and blocking a reopen would be a new way to wedge work.

(b) tools/archiveEpic.ts: the tool promises to be all-or-nothing, and the chokepoint alone would archive the resting tasks before refusing on the finalizing one. Extend the existing safety gate: after the `blocking` in-flight check and before the archive loop, walk the epic's not-yet-archived tasks and throw the same attemptFinalizingRefusal for the first one holding a finalizing attempt. Nothing is written before it.

Error handling: both refusals are thrown MoeErrors (STATE_CONFLICT -32002, retryable), which the MCP adapter forwards as {tool, codeName}; the caller retries after moe.finalize_attempt. Cases 6-7 of step 2 turn green.

Phase 5 — regression and mutation proof, from packages/moe-daemon.

(a) `npx tsc --noEmit` exits 0 and `npm test` is green. The architect's prototype of all five code changes left the suite at 2068 passed | 2 skipped, so the only delta you should see is your new cases. A failure anywhere else means the change reached further than intended — do not paper over it.

(b) Mutation replay in the scratch tree, ONE guard site at a time, restoring from pristine before each (mem:gotcha-backstop-masks-untested-prewrite-guard — a site that turns nothing red is an unpinned site):
- remove the deleteTask close: cases 1-4 red;
- remove the reconcile finalizing branch: case 5 red;
- remove the updateTask terminal guard: case 6 red, and case 7 still green because archive_epic's own check fires first;
- remove archive_epic's check but keep the chokepoint: case 7 red on the sibling task's status (it proves atomicity, not just the refusal);
- remove the DEAD close in deleteWorker: case 8 red; in purgeAllWorkers: case 10 red;
- revert either per-task closeHandedBackAttempts back to closeOpenAttempts: cases 9 and 12 red respectively;
- keep every case's expected reason: an assertion about a phase or a code name, never a missing symbol.
Run an unmutated pass first and last; both must equal the baseline.

(c) Re-run the three suites that touch these paths on their own as well — state/attemptHandback.test.ts, state/workerStore.test.ts, state/sweeps.test.ts — and record the counts.

Record every command, its exit code and its counts in the step note.
